WEBVTT

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:05.880
Picture a five year old on an

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:06.900
engineer's lap,

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:08.990
watching an agent refactor a module.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:10.600
Files open and close,

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:11.510
tests run,

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:12.670
green checks appear.

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:14.140
And the child asks:

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:15.910
is the computer fixing itself?

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:17.580
It's a better question than it sounds,

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:19.740
because the definition of engineering is being

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:20.220
rewritten,

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:21.410
right now.

00:00:22.141 --> 00:00:23.531
The craft is not dying.

00:00:23.541 --> 00:00:24.531
It's molting.

00:00:24.541 --> 00:00:26.411
The outer shell is falling away:

00:00:26.601 --> 00:00:27.581
the keystrokes,

00:00:27.581 --> 00:00:28.431
the syntax,

00:00:28.441 --> 00:00:29.321
the boilerplate.

00:00:29.321 --> 00:00:30.791
But the animal underneath,

00:00:30.861 --> 00:00:32.461
the part that knows what to build

00:00:32.461 --> 00:00:33.941
and knows when it's right,

00:00:33.941 --> 00:00:35.691
is more alive than ever.

00:00:36.437 --> 00:00:37.957
Agents are amplifiers,

00:00:37.957 --> 00:00:39.127
not replacements.

00:00:39.137 --> 00:00:41.157
Give one to a mediocre engineer,

00:00:41.157 --> 00:00:43.837
and you get mediocre software at terrifying

00:00:43.837 --> 00:00:44.557
speed.

00:00:44.557 --> 00:00:46.197
Give one to a great engineer,

00:00:46.197 --> 00:00:47.757
and the old way starts to look

00:00:47.757 --> 00:00:49.717
like building highways with shovels.

00:00:49.717 --> 00:00:51.717
The ones who thrive will master the

00:00:51.717 --> 00:00:52.767
boring things:

00:00:52.997 --> 00:00:53.877
context,

00:00:53.877 --> 00:00:54.947
guardrails,

00:00:55.097 --> 00:00:55.757
tests,

00:00:55.757 --> 00:00:56.607
conventions.

00:00:56.617 --> 00:00:58.607
Tools change every quarter.

00:00:58.977 --> 00:01:00.887
Judgment compounds over a career.

00:01:01.668 --> 00:01:03.098
So this isn't a replacement.

00:01:03.228 --> 00:01:04.148
It's a promotion,

00:01:04.148 --> 00:01:05.668
from typist to captain.

00:01:05.668 --> 00:01:07.528
And the part being automated was never

00:01:07.528 --> 00:01:09.058
the part anyone loved.

00:01:09.308 --> 00:01:11.788
Nobody became an engineer to write boilerplate

00:01:11.788 --> 00:01:12.528
parsers.

00:01:12.528 --> 00:01:14.558
They wanted to build things that matter.

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:16.600
The book is honest about what it

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:17.360
got wrong.

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:19.190
Security was underestimated:

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:21.140
every integration is a channel for someone

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:22.930
else's text to reach the agent.

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:25.290
The bottleneck turned out to be review,

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:26.510
not orchestration.

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:28.670
Agent capacity is cheap.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:30.220
Human attention is not.

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:31.780
And the harness matters as much as

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:32.630
the model.

00:01:33.153 --> 00:01:34.723
Then there's the crew itself.

00:01:34.773 --> 00:01:36.503
A crew suggests loyalty,

00:01:36.513 --> 00:01:38.323
shipmates who know your habits.

00:01:38.533 --> 00:01:40.053
Agents aren't like that,

00:01:40.053 --> 00:01:41.943
they don't remember the last session.

00:01:41.953 --> 00:01:43.543
Each one arrives fresh,

00:01:43.573 --> 00:01:44.573
does its work,

00:01:44.573 --> 00:01:45.523
and disappears.

00:01:45.753 --> 00:01:47.673
It's more like hiring dock workers at

00:01:47.673 --> 00:01:48.523
every port.

00:01:49.163 --> 00:01:50.103
And that's fine.

00:01:50.103 --> 00:01:51.763
For most of maritime history,

00:01:51.763 --> 00:01:53.153
the ship was the continuity.

00:01:53.203 --> 00:01:54.283
The charts,

00:01:54.283 --> 00:01:55.143
the logbook,

00:01:55.143 --> 00:01:55.963
the rigging,

00:01:55.963 --> 00:01:57.153
your code base,

00:01:57.163 --> 00:01:58.013
your conventions,

00:01:58.243 --> 00:01:58.903
your tests,

00:01:58.903 --> 00:01:59.853
and your agents.

00:02:00.343 --> 00:02:01.863
.MD are the ship.

00:02:01.863 --> 00:02:02.783
Build it well,

00:02:02.783 --> 00:02:04.603
and any fresh crew is useful in

00:02:04.603 --> 00:02:05.243
minutes.

00:02:05.243 --> 00:02:06.833
The crew is disposable,

00:02:06.883 --> 00:02:08.053
the ship is not.

00:02:08.758 --> 00:02:09.578
So tonight,

00:02:09.578 --> 00:02:10.678
open a terminal,

00:02:10.678 --> 00:02:12.288
pick the bug you've been avoiding,

00:02:12.318 --> 00:02:13.478
point an agent at it,

00:02:13.478 --> 00:02:14.508
give it context,

00:02:14.678 --> 00:02:15.748
set a guardrail,

00:02:15.758 --> 00:02:16.948
and watch what happens.

00:02:17.018 --> 00:02:19.378
This discipline is being invented right now

00:02:19.378 --> 00:02:20.908
by the people who build.

00:02:20.938 --> 00:02:22.188
You're the captain.

00:02:22.258 --> 00:02:23.258
Next up,

00:02:23.258 --> 00:02:24.588
in the first appendix,

00:02:24.678 --> 00:02:26.508
agents as pen testers.
